# Overview
#
# A filler phrase announces a point instead of making it (`Note that the
# cache is cold`). The reader reads the announcement and learns nothing
# until the point arrives. The fix deletes the phrase and states the
# point (`The cache is cold`). The rule sits at Recommended, because
# each phrase here also has a plain sense that no pattern tells from the
# filler.
#
# Expression
#
# `note that`, `notice that`, and `remember that` count anywhere, except
# where `note` or `notice` is a noun. `before` lists the words that mark
# the noun. It holds the determiners (a determiner is a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`). It also holds the
# tags `ADJ` (an adjective) and `NOUN` (a noun) from Harper's
# part-of-speech tagger. The list names the determiners instead of the
# `DET` tag (a determiner), because the tagger tags `all` in `First of
# all note that` as one.
#
# Weir has no lookbehind. A guard that reads the word before `note`
# fails when no word comes before it, so it misses `Note that` at the
# start of a sentence. The first branch therefore matches the whole noun
# phrase, a noun and the words attached to it. It then narrows the match
# to the empty point before `that`.
#
# Harper reports nothing for an empty match, but it skips every later
# match that starts before that point, so the `note that` inside the
# noun phrase passes.
#
# False Positives
#
# After a determiner, an adjective, or a noun, `note` and `notice` are
# nouns. The `that` after them opens a description (`add a note that
# explains the flag`, `a copyright notice that is included`), so the
# phrase passes.
#
# A commit subject uses `note` as a verb (`docs: note that the flag is
# sticky`), and no pattern tells that verb from the filler that opens
# `Note that the cache is cold`. A sentence can also describe a reader
# or a program that notices or remembers (`you might notice that the
# pods restart`, `the cache must remember that the key expired`). Each
# of those sentences still fires.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseFiller holds `note that`, `notice that`, and
# `remember that`, and the Required ProseFiller holds every other
# announcement. Both tiers match `please note that`, and prose-lint
# prints only the Required finding.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 356 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits announced a point.
expr before [a, an, the, this, each, every, another, your, our, their, its, ADJ, NOUN]
expr noun [note, notice]
expr main [<(@before @noun that), (that), ()>, (note that), (notice that), (remember that)]
let message "Filler phrase. Delete the phrase and state the point."
let description "Flags `note that`, `notice that`, and `remember that`, which announce a point instead of making it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The note field holds free text."
allows "Each note has a title and a body."
allows "Add a note that explains the flag."
allows "The note that the reviewer wrote lists every change."
allows "The license requires a copyright notice that names the author."
